+# Plan: Windows beta adoption
+
+Issue: `1f76ff32-e688-40c2-bb01-6e1392aca45d`
+
+Classification: Standard planning requested for a beta-adoption bug. Delivery is
+split into two small stacked PRs after human approval.
+
+## Research summary
+
+### Repository evidence
+
+| Surface | Evidence at pinned base | Conclusion |
+|---|---|---|
+| Forge checkout EOL | `.gitattributes` has global LF plus explicit shipped-tree rules; `git check-attr` reports LF for shell/plugin paths | Already solved inside Forge's repository |
+| Regression coverage | `test/gitattributes.test.js` checks only source strings and binary declarations | Does not prove consumer setup semantics |
+| Consumer setup | No `.gitattributes` read/write exists in `lib/commands/setup.js` | Remaining root defect |
+| Package contents | `npm pack --dry-run --ignore-scripts` contains `install.sh` and `lefthook.yml`, but not `.gitattributes` | Package install alone cannot apply the consumer rule |
+| Lefthook install | `autoInstallLefthook` is package-manager-aware; missing binary repair and native fallback are tested | Runtime path already exists |
+| Lefthook metadata | optional peer `^1.0.0`, dev dependency `^2.1.4` | Version contract is inconsistent |
+| Windows child cleanup | `scripts/process-tree.js` and runner/fixture tests landed in PRs #479, #495, #513, and #521 | Do not duplicate in this issue |
+| Remaining Node 24 timeout | Kernel issue `54b54219-7cbc-4262-9461-a77112a7c49b`, claimed by `sol-release-timeout` | Separate owner and file set |
+
+Focused baseline command:
+
+```text
+bun test test/gitattributes.test.js test/lefthook-check.test.js test/setup-native-fallback-wiring.test.js
+26 pass, 0 fail, 8.92s
+```
+
+### Primary references
+
+- Git attributes: `text` normalizes the index, `eol=lf` controls checkout, and
+  existing content is renormalized with an explicit `git add --renormalize`:
+  https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes
+- Lefthook's supported Node-package install and package choices:
+  https://lefthook.dev/installation/node/
+- Lefthook install behavior and automatic NPM-package hook installation:
+  https://lefthook.dev/usage/commands/install/
+- Bun test timeouts are per-test and child processes require bounded ownership:
+  https://bun.sh/docs/test/writing-tests
+- Bun subprocesses support explicit timeout/kill behavior:
+  https://bun.sh/docs/runtime/child-process
+
+## Recommended implementation
+
+Do not add another global rule to Forge's own `.gitattributes`; it already works.
+Propagate the LF contract across the actual product boundary by safely merging a
+small managed block into the consumer repository during normal setup. Keep
+renormalization user-reviewed. Align the optional Lefthook peer range and prove
+the existing install/fallback behavior instead of inventing a second installer.
+
+## Stacked PR decomposition
+
+### Stack W1 — consumer LF contract
+
+Purpose: close the actual setup boundary defect.
+
+Owned files:
+
+- `lib/gitattributes-wiring.js` (new, pure managed-block merge)
+- `lib/commands/setup.js` (one orchestration call)
+- `test/gitattributes-wiring.test.js` (new unit contract)
+- `test/setup-gitattributes.test.js` (new live setup integration contract)
+
+No other stack edits these files. Start from the approved planning commit, rebase
+on exact current master immediately before development, and keep this PR free of
+package metadata and general timeout work.
+
+Exit evidence:
+
+- RED: live setup fixture lacks a consumer `.gitattributes` LF result.
+- GREEN: `core.autocrlf=true` fixture reports `eol: lf`; preservation,
+  idempotence, and `--skills-only` cases pass.
+- strict lint and diff check pass; no auto-staging occurs.
+
+### Stack W2 — Lefthook adoption contract and user guidance
+
+Base: exact merged W1 head. It may be developed only after W1's public contract
+is stable.
+
+Owned files:
+
+- `package.json` (align optional peer range only)
+- `bun.lock` only if the package manager deterministically changes it
+- `test/cross-platform-install.test.js`
+- `test/lefthook-check.test.js`
+- `docs/guides/SETUP.md`
+- `docs/reference/TOOLCHAIN.md`
+
+Exit evidence:
+
+- RED: package contract test exposes the `^1.0.0` / `^2.1.4` mismatch.
+- GREEN: all four package-manager command paths, Windows binary forms, and
+  native fallback remain covered.
+- Packaging dry-run proves intended shipped files; docs explain the hook verdict
+  and optional reviewed renormalization command.
+- Reuse the existing Windows CI matrix; do not create another workflow.
+
+### Parallel but explicitly separate
+
+Issue `54b54219-7cbc-4262-9461-a77112a7c49b` owns the deterministic Windows
+Node 24 pre-push timeout. Its likely surfaces (`scripts/test.js`,
+`scripts/test-full-suite.js`, `scripts/process-tree.js`, and
+`test/scripts/test-runner.test.js`) are forbidden in W1/W2. This lets its owner
+proceed in parallel without merge conflict or duplicated diagnosis.
+
+Open PR file checks also show no overlap with #514 (Flow package), #522 (release
+writer/reference), or #523 (Kernel claim projection).
+
+## Validation strategy
+
+1. Unit-test managed-block insertion, replacement, newline preservation,
+   malformed/missing file behavior, and idempotence.
+2. Run the live setup handler in a temporary Git repository with
+   `core.autocrlf=true`; use `git check-attr`, not string inspection, as the
+   behavioral oracle.
+3. Verify setup leaves the index and unrelated working-tree files unchanged.
+4. Exercise Lefthook checker/install command selection without network access;
+   use fixture binaries for `lefthook`, `.cmd`, `.exe`, and `.bunx`.
+5. Run focused tests and strict lint locally, then the existing Windows CI lane.
+6. If Windows CI times out outside the owned files, hand the receipt to issue
+   `54b54219-7cbc-4262-9461-a77112a7c49b`; do not raise timeouts in this stack.
+
+## Risks and mitigations
+
+| Risk | Mitigation |
+|---|---|
+| Existing user attribute rules are overwritten | Delimited append/replace only; preservation tests |
+| Setup unexpectedly stages a mature repo | Pure filesystem merge; explicit no-index-change test |
+| Later user rule overrides Forge LF | Append managed block at EOF and document ownership |
+| Broad `*.sh` changes an intentional CRLF policy | Human scope gate; recommend broad rule because POSIX shell requires LF reliability |
+| Setup downloads tools unexpectedly | Preserve current behavior and `--skills-only`; network-independent tests |
+| Windows flake gets hidden by budget increases | No global timeout changes; separate issue owns diagnosis |
+
+## Approval checkpoint
+
+Development must not start until the four gates in `design.md` are answered.
+After approval, record the decisions in `decisions.md`, then run W1 before W2.
